Think of BindingResult as a summary of all validation errors.
It holds:
    What fields had issues
    What were the error messages
    What values were rejected

Because we want field-specific messages, not just one general message so bindingResult.getFieldErrors()
Returns a list of errors like: categoryName → "must not be blank" | categoryId → "must be greater than 0" etc.

| Aspect                    | `ResponseStatusException`                                                   | Custom Exception (`MyCustomException`)                         |
| ------------------------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------------- | -------------------------------------------------------------- |
| ✅ Usage                  | Quickly throw an error with status + message                                | Define reusable, domain-specific exceptions                    |
| 🔧 Flexibility            | Limited (no custom fields unless extended)                                  | High — can add custom fields, logic, etc.                      |
| 📦 Built-in               | Yes (from `org.springframework.web.server`)                                 | No — you define your own                                       |
| 🌐 Mapping to HTTP status | Directly includes `HttpStatus`                                              | Requires `@ExceptionHandler` or `@ResponseStatus`              |
| 🧠 Ideal for...           | Simple validation or missing resources                                      | Business/domain errors (e.g., `InsufficientBalanceException`)  |
| 🧵 Stack trace clarity    | Can feel generic in logs                                                    | Clear exception class name in trace                            |
| 👨‍💻 Example usage          | `throw new ResponseStatusException(HttpStatus.NOT_FOUND, "User not found")` | `throw new UserNotFoundException("User with ID 10 not found")` |

| Part                    | What Happens                            |
| ----------------------- | --------------------------------------- |
| `super(message)`        | Stores the message inside the exception |
| `e.getMessage()`        | Retrieves that message in the handler   |
| `ResponseEntity.body()` | Sends that message in the HTTP response |


